.BEACHES...
Issued at 211 PM PST Wed Nov 18 2026

For the High Surf Advisory:

A High Surf Advisory will go into effect at 4 PM this afternoon
and continue through 4 AM Friday with breaking waves of 18 to 24
ft with up to 30 ft and favored break points. The risk of sneaker
waves and strong rip currents will persist as well. These waves
are less energetic than the ones we saw on this past Saturday,
which ended up flooding portions of Pacifica and damaging Capitola
Pier, but remain hazardous to potential swimmers, could still
knock unaware people into the water, and continue to feed an
increased risk of rip currents. The risk of drowning remains high.
Do not go in the water, avoid climbing on the rocks or leaning
over seawalls, and never turn your back on the ocean.

For the Coastal Flood Advisory:

A combination of high astronomical tides and thermal expansion
from warmer than normal waters will result in tidal flooding
concerns across Bayshore locations in the San Francisco Bay and
San Pablo Bay. At the San Francisco tidal gauge, high tide is
expected to be 1.2 ft above normal (7.0 ft MLLW) at 7:15 AM
Thursday and 1.1 ft above normal (6.9 ft MLLW) at 7:45 AM Friday.
These predictions include up to 1.8 ft of surge that will enhance
the astronomical tide and flooding threat. High tide varies up to
90 minutes earlier or later along the Pacific Coast and through
the San Francisco Bay, respectively. Coastal flooding will likely
continue into the weekend with the potential to add the low-lying
areas of the Pacific Coast as we approach King Tides November
24-26.

